  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-test/an-agents-links-pull-from-its-root seon.task-test/an-agents-live-state-is-facts-on-its-entity seon.procs-test/root-records-what-it-observes-of-its-agents seon.agent-test/a-resumed-branchs-agent-is-this-boots}}
 {:seon.task/name "walk"
  :seon.task/plan "The one mechanism every view of Seon's data goes through: the owner's window, root's page and the model's context (owner, 2026-10-23: \"locations should be based on a starting EID and we pull from there to render the data what one hop away\"; \"I want logical solution for everything so we aren't inventing new shit\"; \"rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"). What v1 did (~/src/seon/resources/seon/schemas/seon.agent.edn :agent, render/walk.clj neighborhood at :seon.render/distance 1): an entity's schema declares the neighbours a view of it shows as :seon.render/units, attributes in pull's own syntax (a leading _ the reverse ref), components included; the walk pulls the entity and those attributes, and each neighbour renders by its own schema's render. Bring the rule, not v1's 1263 lines (most are SCI caps and caches). Build: (1) :seon.render/units on the schemas this needs: the agent's (:seon.agent/agent-fact: its run :seon.run/_agent, its evaluations :seon.eval/_agent, its root), the run's (:seon.run/run-fact: its task, its turns :seon.turn/_run, the messages to it :seon.message/_to), the task's (its plan, proofs), root's (:seon.root/root-fact: its agents :seon.agent/_root, the open problems); (2) seon.render/walk: one d/pull of an eid with its own attributes and its units (on a branch: the branch's db, or seon.union's U for what spans branches, ids issued once store-wide, docs/storage.md#ids), plus what its string attributes cite (seon.ref/parse, each citation pulled at the depth its delimiter asks, as seon.context pulls it), answering the tree as one map (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We are returning a giant map right? so we can show what rendered it, what it is (a doc a symbol to a file, etc ?)\"): each node its value and what it is (an entity and its schema, a var, a doc section path#anchor, a file path:line, a citation found in a string), where it came from (eid and attribute, or the string and citation that pulled it) and the render that showed it with its :as, so an agent reading it knows what to change: the producer, the render or the citation. Every key in that map is fully namespaced and has a registered schema (owner, 2026-10-23: \"we only use fully namespaced schemas and we don't want to be cute with :you stuff\"): a pulled entity keeps its own attributes, and the walk's own parts are schemas of seon.render (its references, each citation's place, which render made which part). A citation's content is pulled once per context and cited by its number where it appears, so two citations of one var never repeat it; What each kind of value the walk pulls costs and who needs it (owner via root, 2026-10-23: \"I want each piece of data we are processing to be looked at and determined if we should just be dumping the whole thing in without regard to whether it's relevant\"), measured as the model's text on one run (the /walk root, task seon.render/config-shows-where-each-value-came-from), before -> after: root's config (its 108 :seon.config/* attributes, a 14 KB value) 1,801 -> 0 B: nobody needs it whole, the model needs the keys its task's code reads; root's focused render (seon.render.ai/root-fact) shows its boot, generation and how it last stopped, and a config key reaches a context only as one the task's code reads (to build); root 87 B, one line; an agent 386 -> 94 B: status, pid, branch and the form running (seon.render.ai/agent-fact), its description only for an agent no run started, cut at the producer (seon.root/observed: a run's agent's :task was its task id, which pulled the whole task into every walk of the agent a second time, 375 B -> 0); a run 753 -> 159 B: id, status or outcome and reason, model, turn of max, branch (seon.render.ai/run-fact); its plan is its task's and its boot root's, both cut; a task 906-1,265 B, its plan 263 B and proofs 53 B: shown whole, the model's work is in them; a cited var at its citation's depth 605 B: whole, the depth chooses it. Totals: an agent's walk 3,920 -> 945 B; root's 2,594 -> 703 B; a run's 2,589 -> 2,042 B. Not on this run, to measure when seon.context/evaluations records them: turns, evaluations, messages, problems, doc sections, schema forms. Calls for the owner: whether config shows the keys the task's code reads (a reach over the :seon.config/* keywords its forms name) or only a key a text cites; whether a task as a run's neighbour is whole or its plan and proofs only; root's walk shows every boot's agents (two for one run after a restart): this boot's only, or ended ones collapsed into one line. prose is markdown and facts are data (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Somethings are better as data and some are better as markdown\"): a value whose schema is prose (help, a plan, a doc section, a message, a reply) renders as markdown and is parsed for its citations; an entity, proofs, cost, the references and the provenance render as data an agent can read back and use; each value's render decides by its schema, so one evaluation may hold both; (3) the tree printed by the one printer (seon.print with seon.render/nested): the root of the walk by its :html render :as :block, each unit :as :tile; a page part is drawn by its :html render alone, never its :ai face (task a-page-part-renders-only-its-view). What is not useful is cut at its producer or given a focused render on its schema, never filtered by a view (owner: \"if shit isn't useful we either cut it at the producer or make specific renders to help focus the context or html\"). Read first: docs/render.md, seon.render (render-of, nested, print-ai), seon.print, seon.ref, seon.context's pulls (what it pulls today and at which depth), v1's walk. Replaces nothing yet: page and evaluations delete the hand-built producers."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/the-agents-links-are-refs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-html-fallback-folds-only-what-nests seon.task-test/a-new-boot-leaves-the-earlier-boots-agents}}
 {:seon.task/name "page-walks-only-the-rows-shown"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a page renders only its first rows (`seon.render.ui/page`, `:seon.config.render.list/history`), but every pass walks every row first: `seon.render/page` walks the entity on main and on its branch, each unit whole (`seon.render/walk` pulls every evaluation and its record), measured at 5 contracted calls and about 41 KB an evaluation a pass (`seon.render-cost-test/a-page-walks-each-db-once`: 41 MB at 1,000), and root's page shows every agent of its boot as a tile, unpaged. Correct: a unit shown as rows is pulled newest first only as far as the rows shown (pull's :limit on the reverse ref, or an index read of the newest), its count read apart, so a pass costs the rows on screen, not the list; root's tiles paged like the list. Measure first: the transactions of the newest rows by index, against the pull."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/page"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-cost-test/a-page-walks-only-the-rows-shown}}
 {:seon.task/name "page"
  :seon.task/plan "Every page is the walk of one entity (seon.render/walk) through its :html face (owner, 2026-10-23: \"no separate run view it's part of the agents view\"; \"root is also an agents view, it just has different focus since it should be linked up with all the agents it's running on the system, the system health, etc\"). What exists: seon.render.proc/zoom-value assembles an agent's window by hand from root's view, main's run and the branch, joined by strings (turn-rows, eval-records, a tile list, a run-or-agent branch, a :turn-ctx cache), the /run/{id} route beside /agent/{id}, and root's page a second producer (seon.render.ui/system-page, agent-card and the grid). Build: a location is an entity (/agent/{id} resolves to the agent's eid in root's boot, / to root's); the page is the walk: the entity as the main pane (:block), its units the column (:tile) in their declared order, each a link that moves the eid; a unit is rendered again when a commit changes its entity (the render proc's events), never polled. The owner's window is the same evaluations the model's context shows (seon.context/evaluations), through the :html face: one list, newest at the top, each evaluation rendered once (its thought, its form, its result by the result's own render, its cost), a model turn grouping its evaluations under its header; no second output (no transcript beside a column of the same results), and no special place for now: it is the newest evaluation (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I'm wondering if we don't just limit it to now and instead it's every eval that renders to html sorted by newest at the top\"; \"no I don't want separate outputs I want responsive html and css so why not tailwind?\"). Layout is responsive CSS: a render tags its root with how it is shown (:as) and Tailwind's classes lay it out at each width; the content a render includes is its own choice by :as (an :inline value is one line, for the :ai face too). Tailwind as v1 built it, ported to Jolt's build (owner, 2026-10-23: \"look at the ~/src/seon I think we had something for tailwind generation? but build it for the jolt build process so it's simple and we can just update the css and it regenerrates without a lot of fuss\"): v1's ~/src/seon/bin/css is a two-argument transform, Tailwind v4 CSS-first (every directive, token and source glob in resources/public/css/input.css, no config file), measured 171-187 ms, a build product and never a runtime component (v1 deleted its Integrant watcher). Keep v1's lessons: `@import \"tailwindcss\" source(none)` with explicit `@source` globs over src/**/*.clj (auto-detection walked the reference-code tree: 42 s, against ~1 s), and a safelist of the utility vocabulary agents may use, since a render an agent defines at its REPL is never in a scanned file. On Jolt: Tailwind's standalone v4 binary in place of bun and node_modules, pinned by version and checksum, fetched into target/ and run as a step of bin/build (and so of the :build effect after merges), its output reused by the digest of input.css and the scanned sources (AGENTS §5): edit input.css or a render's classes, build, and the stylesheet is regenerated; unchanged inputs regenerate nothing. The hand-written app.css goes. The chat input (evaluate, message, interrupt, release) stays at the top, where the newest is. Only what is on screen is rendered (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We can dynamically load the tiles and not show the ones offscreen right?\"): the page carries the newest units and a placeholder saying how many older there are; when it scrolls into view (Datastar's data-on-intersect) the render proc renders the next ones, so the server works only for what is looked at, by that event, never a poll. Deletes zoom-value, turn-rows, eval-records' joins, the /run route, system-page's hand-built sections, agent-card and the grid (one :tile render of an agent replaces both), the tile list and :turn-ctx. A browser subagent compares each page against the facts (docs/checks.md#checking-a-page-in-a-browser). The old window's last browser round (2026-10-23, agent a5) found what the new page must not repeat: a count of evaluations the list does not show (every evaluation is a row, or the count is not shown); a name shortened without saying so (the branch's :main/ prefix); a tile that says 0 without saying nothing has run; numbers without their unit or label, and a warning without its bound."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/walk"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/root-and-an-agent-are-one-chrome seon.render-test/a-units-change-rerenders-that-unit seon.render-cost-test/a-page-walks-each-db-once}}
