 {:seon.task/name "a-view-renders-once-per-change-of-its-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 2 of docs/render.md (revision 3), the foundation; design section 3 (Render each thing once; Components, reads and patches) and root's research (datahike-reads.md). Four parts, each merged with its proofs.
(A) Read recording in the Datahike fork, branch jolt/with-reads of seantempesta/datahike (pinned at reference-code/datahike, today 698a2faf). Reuses the one index interface every read bottoms out in (IIndex, index/persistent_set.cljc -slice, -rslice, -lookup; -all, -seq, -count record a wildcard). Adds a dynamic collector `*reads*` there (one vswap! a range, [index from to] as the slice is made, so laziness after the call is fine); db/search.cljc memoize-for and the query result cache bypassed while recording (both off on root today); `datahike.query/*disable-planner*` bound while recording (the planner's ~25 cursor calls skip the interface; the relational engine is faster on point and 2-hop queries, 2.6x slower on a 392-row join). API in datahike.api: `(d/with-reads f)` answers [result reads]; `(d/reads-index reads)` keys each range by its bound prefix ([:e e] [:ea e a] [:a a] [:av a v], or any); `(d/reads-touched? idx tx-data)` one hash lookup a datom, added or retracted, a schema datom touching all. Deletes nothing. ~35 lines, Datahike's tests beside them. Proofs (fork): with-reads records 3 ranges for a pull with one nested ref, 1 for a unique point query, 1 for an attribute scan; reads-touched? is true for a datom in a read range and false outside it, for an addition and a retraction; cost: reads-touched? costs the transaction's datoms, flat in the number of reads.
(B) One render cache keyed by reads, in seon.render.proc (its state, no new namespace), whose components are today's pages, one per db a page reads (root's ruling 2026-10-24, option 2: per-entity components are step 4's, task a-page-is-its-entitys-view, on this cache): each watched page's walk on main and on its agent's branch runs inside d/with-reads, and the page keeps each db's reads index; an inverted index per db (key -> views, from d/datom-keys) finds the pages a transaction touched in O(its datoms). A touched page walks again and its units re-render only when their input changed (step-units' comparison stays until step 4). Reuses render/page, step-units, finish-patch. Deletes nothing yet. ~50 lines. Proofs: seon.render-test/a-page-keeps-what-it-read (a page's reads hold its entity and its rows' entities; a datom of another entity is not among them).
(C) Root's render proc walks and renders only the pages a transaction touched. Main: the writer's report, connected [:writer :main] -> [:render :main] ({:t :ops :db}, as the reconciler, prove and vectors take it), replacing the :state answer's tx-count compare and its :branch-db ask for main. An agent's branch: its :commit event (exists) asks the branch db as today (ask-branches), and the datoms that moved are (d/datoms-diff held new :eavt []) (O(changed), as merge.clj and program.clj use it). Deletes: the pass that walks every watched page per event: an event or transaction touching no page's reads walks and renders nothing. Root's system value and its :ai context stay a pass per state answer (not a page; out of this step). ~40 lines changed. Proofs: seon.render-test/a-transaction-touching-no-page-walks-nothing, seon.render-test/a-branch-commit-touches-its-page-by-its-datoms-diff; cost: seon.render-cost-test/a-transaction-costs-its-datoms-to-match (finding the touched pages: linear in the datoms, flat in the pages watched).
(D) Agents commit after each eval: seon.turn/act!'s call loop commits (seon.agent/commit!) after each call's record, so a page sees an eval as it lands (design section 11 item 1, approved). Reuses commit! (no-op when nothing waits); the commit at idle and turn end stays (it covers writes outside an eval). ~3 lines. Proof: seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval (process test: two calls, two :commit events before the turn's end). Measured: a commit's cost (ticks, bytes) per eval, before and after, reported with the step; if it is past the eval's own cost, root decides before merging.
What it does not do: per-entity components and the agent page and screen as views (step 4, task a-page-is-its-entitys-view), recording what a turn sent (step 3), the browser's lazy placeholders (design section 4). Estimated src: +~90 (fork ~65 outside src), -~30. Checked in a browser: root's page and an agent's page update as an agent evaluates, with the render proc's counts showing hits."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/a-page-part-renders-only-its-view"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.reads-test/a-read-records-what-it-scanned
                      datahike.reads-test/a-transaction-touches-only-what-a-read-scanned
                      datahike.reads-test/a-read-inside-a-flow-step-is-recorded
                      datahike.reads-test/a-transaction-costs-its-datoms-to-match
                      datahike.reads-test/matching-is-flat-in-what-was-read
                      seon.render-test/a-page-keeps-what-it-read
                      seon.render-test/a-transaction-touching-no-page-walks-nothing
                      seon.render-test/a-branch-commit-touches-its-page-by-its-datoms-diff
                      seon.render-cost-test/a-transaction-costs-its-datoms-to-match
                      seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-page-is-its-entitys-view"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 4 of docs/render.md (revision 3) with what steps 2 and 3 deferred into it and what the debug page showed (root, 2026-10-24). Too big for one step: five parts, each its own report and proofs, in this order (4a is the foundation of the rest; 4d and 4e need only 4c).
(4a) Per-entity components on step 2's cache. A component is a view of one entity on one db, id e<eid>, rendered inside d/with-reads (its view reads its entity through pull and q, realized), held in the render proc's cache with its reads indexed (step 2's :read-index, keyed now by component, not page); a transaction re-renders exactly the components whose reads it touched, once, and the patch goes to every page showing that component (the web proc's fan-out, keyed by component id). A page is its entity's component: its view reads its own attributes and its neighbours' ids (the reverse refs it lists) and calls each neighbour's view ([view-fn eid] in its hiccup, as Reagent does); a neighbour already on the page shows as a link. Rows are ordered by entity id (issued in the order written), not by a datoms read per row (which made any row's change touch the page). Reuses: d/with-reads, :read-index and touched, step-units' patch shapes and containers, ui/view, the schema-declared views, seon.web/chunk-of, the windowing (:shown, ui/more). Deletes: render/walk's whole-unit pulls, render/merged and render/page (the page is its entity's view), ui/rows, tile and head as separate unit kinds, step-units' input comparison, extra-of's page-level inputs (each becomes its component's: scroll and open on the list component, liveness on the agent's), debug-value's held-input reuse (a turn is a component, written once and never touched again). The debug page's ~34 KB per held turn goes: a new turn renders its own component and appends to the list. ~150 lines changed (render.clj, render/proc.clj, render/ui.clj), ~200 deleted. Proofs: seon.render-test/an-unchanged-unit-never-re-renders (a transaction touching one eval re-renders that component and the list holding it; counts), a-new-evaluation-is-one-append, a-component-on-two-pages-renders-once (root's page and an agent's page share the agent's component: one render, two patches); cost: seon.render-cost-test/a-transaction-renders-what-it-touched (renders: the touched components, flat in the page's rows), beside a-page-walks-each-db-once (re-estimated); a-new-turn-costs-one-turns-messages re-estimated flat in held turns.
(4b) /agent/{id} is the agent entity's view as the chat page (design section 5). Sidebar: the agent's ref to root, rendered (root's view lists its agents' components; CSS shows them small here). Center: the conversation in tx order, the model's prose and reasoning as markdown (the existing prose render), each eval a collapsed line (its code's first line, its cost) that opens to its form and response (step 3's /open toggle), the user's messages and forms in the same timeline; the input box takes prose, or a form when it reads as one (seon.root/submit! or message!, as today). Tiles on the right, newest first: one per eval whose value has a declared render, and the task's proofs and the branch's changes (4c). The user is an agent: an agent entity with no process (:seon.agent/id user), written by root at boot (design section 2); evals and messages it makes name it (:seon.eval/by its id, as they name user today). Reuses: 4a's components, the eval and message renders, agent-input, prose. Deletes: the agent page's separate head, rows and tiles layout (ui/page for agents). New: the user agent entity at boot (no new attribute). ~120 lines. Proofs: seon.render-test/the-agent-page-is-its-conversation (order, prose as markdown, an eval collapsed then open), the-sidebar-is-roots-agents (the same agent components as root's page); checked in a browser on a scripted run.
(4c) The screen is a read of that page. (seon.screen/show) asks root (the agent's existing request channel) for the agent page's cached hiccup without its chat column, presentation attributes removed (class, style, data-*: one small function over hiccup), at main's head, recorded with its t (so a turn's screen reads back from records); it is the only screen form. my.task/status and my.branch/changes stop being screen evals: their content is the page's tiles (the task's proofs with status, failure and how to run each; the branch's changes against main), so nothing is shown twice. Reuses: 4a/4b's components, root's request path, seon.screen/forms (one form). Deletes: my.task/status and my.branch/changes as screen evals (they stay REPL functions), and the turn sections the tiles replace (seon.context/turn-state's :proofs, :refused, :test-run, :changes, :attention) once step 6's measure shows nothing lost. ~60 lines. Proofs: (the hiccup equals the page component's, chat column gone, no presentation attributes), nothing-is-shown-twice (no eval or proof in both the conversation and the screen).
(4d) What the agent is told about its task (task-check.md in root's scratchpad; the debug page). The task's data (the [:task eid t] part) shows: the plan; the write set, the vars the plan cites, each with its source and its callers (my.agent/callers); each proof with the form that runs it at the REPL, or, when it is not written, that it is not and what it should assert (the plan's words). Whether and why a proof fails (its first failing assertion) is the screen's proofs tile, from the newest turn's basis: the task's data is the run's cached prefix, the same all run, and a failure changes as the agent works (built 2026-10-24). It stops showing :found (helpers by embedding: unrelated test source in every measured run), :map (the namespaces' map) and :evidence unless the plan cites them. The screen (4c) adds what the agent has done this run: the vars it read (source, docs), defined and evaluated, and the tests it ran with their results, from the run's eval records, so a repeated read shows as a repeat. Reuses: task-data (narrowed), docs/resolve-ref, callers, the proof facts, the run's eval records. Deletes from task-keys: :found, :map, :evidence (seon.context/found and ns-map with their cost proofs if nothing else reads them), :sections unless cited. ~80 lines changed, ~120 deleted. Proofs: seon.context-test/the-task-data-is-plan-write-set-and-proofs (no :found; each cited var's source and callers; each proof's run form and failure or not-written note), the-screen-says-what-the-run-did. Measured in step 6, not claimed better before.
(4e) The eval tool takes {prose, code}, prose first, both required: done in the prototype (seon.model/eval-tool, :required [prose code]). Left: prose is the user's markdown in the chat (4b), and what prose cites is parsed once when the eval is recorded (the one reference parser) and stored in its record, so the screen (4c) renders the cited things (a var's card, a task's plan and proofs, a wiki section) after the page, within the budget, instead of each read parsing again. Reuses: seon.ref/references, docs/cited-view, the eval record. Deletes: the per-render reference parse of prose (design section 8: 1.66M ticks a string). New: the eval record holds its prose's references (inside its EDN record, no new attribute). ~40 lines. Proofs: seon.context-test/prose-references-are-parsed-once (recorded with the eval; a render reads them, never the parser), the-screen-shows-what-the-prose-cites.
No part needs a paid run (step 6 measures). New attributes: none."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.context/a-turn-records-what-it-sent"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/an-unchanged-unit-never-re-renders
                      seon.render-test/a-new-evaluation-is-one-append
                      seon.render-cost-test/a-transaction-renders-what-it-touched
                      seon.render-test/the-agent-page-is-its-conversation
                      seon.render-test/the-sidebar-is-roots-agents
                      seon.context-test/the-task-data-is-plan-write-set-and-proofs
                      seon.context-test/prose-references-are-parsed-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-an-agent-types-reads-as-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 6's runs (2026-10-25, the scratch paper measure/step6 (git history)) read helpers by a doseq over quoted names and by #'f, and `my.agent/source-fn` answered invalid-input each time; an eval that printed and returned nil recorded :line \"\" (`seon.repl/record-details`), so its collapsed row showed nothing. Now `my.agent/named` takes a symbol or a var for source-fn, callers and callees; the source macro evaluates any form that is not a bare global name; record-details' line of a nil value is \"nil\". Reuses: the one printer, clojure.repl's interning of my.agent's source. ~20 lines."
