 {:seon.task/name "a-render-defect-answers-the-pages-it-holds"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-26 on the live-agents root (port 8541): after /agent/a5/debug was opened, every page (/, /agent/a7, each debug page) hung past 20 s with no bytes while /render.edn and the static files answered. The render proc's ping showed :passes frozen at 1344 and :pending 0, and root's problems one :platform row: :seon.root/proc-defect from seon.render.proc/render-proc, \"No matching clause: :line\", 23 times. The cause of that throw is fixed (seon.render.ui/source had no clause for a [:line id] part, seon.render-test/every-sent-part-names-its-record). The hang is the mechanism's: a pass that throws keeps the proc's old state (Flow's step catch), so the pages in its :waiting are never answered and every later pass throws the same way; the api answers the message the render proc threw on once more (seon.root's :back error handling, task seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took), which throws again; the web proc's promises for those pages wait forever (AGENTS §9: a wait with no end). Correct: a render proc defect answers the pages it holds, each with the defect (a 500 naming it), never left waiting; one view's throw does not stop the others' answers. Options for root's gate: (a) the api, on {:op :error :pid :render}, sends the web proc the defect and the web answers every page promise it holds with it (a new edge api -> web in system.edn); (b) the render proc renders each view apart and a view whose render throws answers its page with the error, a named failure point now that agents write renders (docs/failure-points.md). What v1 did and the field's way to be researched first. Proofs: a render proc whose pass throws answers a waiting page with the defect; the other views of that pass still answer. Root's gate (2026-10-26): (b). Built: seon.render.proc/view-pass, a named failure point; the error names the view, what threw and the function it threw in (seon.error/in from the exception), not a REPL form, since the view's render state lives in the render proc and no REPL reaches it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-view-that-throws-answers-its-page-with-the-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-and-agents-one-chat-with-a-context-toggle"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-26: \"I want the webui working too for root [and] agents and both having a debug option to see their actual context, otherwise I want a nice chat UI with all the evals that have render functions having the live tiles for them.\" Root's page and an agent's are one chat (seon.render.ui/chat): the same sidebar, root first then its agents (component :side reads root's entity too), the same eval blocks. The top bar toggles each to what its model reads (seon.render.ui/top-bar): /agent/{id}/debug as before; /root/debug the context the render proc holds each pass (its :context, the system's :ai face, the text /context.txt serves), drawn by seon.render.ui/root-context, never computed for the page. Live tiles are left to the joint design (docs/render.md): every eval shows its current value, and whether its form is replayed each turn is an explicit attribute, never inferred from the value (owner, 2026-10-26). Replaces: the agent input's link to its debug page (the toggle). Left for later: an eval line on root's page (root's REPL is the server's root-eval; the web's requests reach agents' processes, not root's), a task for root-as-one-system. docs/render.md#the-page."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/roots-context-is-its-debug-page}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-ctx-is-shown-as-its-parts"
  :seon.task/plan "Slice 2a of the one derivation (docs/render.md, revision 3, root's approval of 2026-10-26): the pure functions the page and the model are drawn from. (seon.render/render-ctx ctx) answers an agent's ctx as ordered keyed parts [{:key k :value v}]: [:task] (the task's relevant keys), [:proofs] (each proof's status and first failure, from the newest turn's observed results), [:run] (the newest run's id, status, turn, max turns, outcome and reason), then the conversation: [:eval id] per eval and [:message id] per message, in the order they were written. It is pure in ctx: equal ctx, equal parts; it reads no db and no config. Noise stays out of the parts: no :db/id, no :t or :commit, no :seon.agent/answered-t, no turn basis but the newest's observed results (the owner: every change is told, so a change without meaning is a data defect). (seon.render/faces part) answers {:html :text}, both default faces from one selection of the one printer (seon.print.select/show with :outputs #{:text :hiccup}, where render/html and render/ai select twice today, render.clj:113-132), a schema's :seon.render/html or :seon.render/ai overriding its face alone. (seon.render/drawn memo part f) memoises a face per part key, a hit when the fn the var holds now and the value are both identical? (a redefined fn draws again). Replaces nothing yet: 2b moves the agent page onto these parts and deletes what they replace."
